.TH CONN 1 "" "conn 0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- the station from which one operator directs the work of many hands on one machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme claude
.br
.B conn theme vim
.br
.B conn \-\-light
.br
.B conn \-\-dark
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
A hand is a process that works a place on the operator's behalf: a shell, an
agent, a run. conn shows what each hand is doing and which hand is waiting, and
puts the operator in front of the one that is.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBPURPOSE.\fR conn is the station from which one operator directs the work of
many hands on one machine. A hand is a process that works a place on the
operator's behalf: a shell, an agent, a run. Hands work without direction, and
stop to ask for it. The station shows what each hand is doing and which hand is
waiting, so that the operator's attention, which is the one thing not in supply,
goes where it is asked for.
.TP
1-2.
\fBTHE HANDS.\fR The kernel's word is process, and it is too small for the thing
being run. The word here is hand, and where that could be read as the operator's
own, station hand: the general worker on a station, who does what the place
needs without being stood over. A hand is long-lived. It has a place it works, a
tree of processes under it, and, if it is an agent, a conversation it is
carrying and a state of mind that cannot be read off its processor time. Above
all it can stop and wait on the operator. A compiler never waited on anyone.
That one new fact is why the hands are the thing to watch, and why the question
is no longer whether a thing is done but whether it needs you.
.TP
1-3.
\fBFILES.\fR Tooling grew up around files and the things done to them, because
the person did the work and a process was a brief event, run and watched to its
end. The files are still where the work lands. They are now what the hands are
working on, the way they used to be what the operator was working on, and the
operator goes to them to review what a hand did or to read before answering it.
An editor is a place visited from the station, not the place the operator lives.
.TP
1-4.
\fBTHE TWO QUESTIONS.\fR Everything on the screen answers one of two questions
the operator keeps asking: what is going on, and who needs me. The watch answers
the first. The waiting word, its blink, \fBtab\fR, and the bar's lamps answer
the second. Every other rule in this manual, the order that holds still, the
words that are earned, the panel that stays dark, the one orange, exists so that
those two answers can be taken in at a glance rather than read.
.TP
1-5.
\fBTHE NAME.\fR The conn is the Navy's word for control of a ship's movement.
The officer who has it does not work the ship; the hands do. The officer
receives reports and gives orders. conn is named for that seat and not for the
machine.
.TP
1-6.
\fBWHAT IS SAID.\fR conn says only what it knows to be true, in a word it has
earned, and says nothing where it knows nothing. A guess is worse than silence.
Nothing is said twice and nothing is said of what there is none of. A row that
always says something is a row nobody reads.
.TP
1-7.
\fBCOMMANDS.\fR conn alone comes up on the console and continues to the watch.
The rest are said by name after it. \fB\-\-light\fR and \fB\-\-dark\fR say the
ground instead of asking the terminal, and go ahead of a command if there is
one.
.PP
.I "SYNOPSIS"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn\fR
The console, then the watch.
.TP
\fBconn down\fR
Takes the server down, with everything in it, and says what went.
.TP
\fBconn theme claude\fR
Writes conn's theme for Claude Code, which draws its own colors.
.TP
\fBconn theme vim\fR
Writes conn's colorscheme for nvim.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-light\fR
Says the ground is light, for a server coming up or one already up.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-dark\fR
Says the ground is dark.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-help\fR
Says this table.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-version\fR
Says the build.
.RE
